<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Blindness of both eyes since childhood (H54.0X55)</content>. Ophthalmology finds comfortable, quiet eyes with no active disease. Because there is no light perception and no pain, lowering eye pressure cannot help his vision, so routine pressure checks, imaging, and visual fields are not needed. No eye drops are needed. [S]<list><item>Yearly ophthalmology review, or the same day for eye pain, redness, discharge, or headache around the eye.</item><item>Primary care asks about eye comfort at each visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Opioid use disorder, in sustained remission (F11.21)</content>. Sustained remission continues, with no cravings. Naloxone has been dispensed with a braille label. [S]<list><item>Keep one naloxone spray at home and one in his travel bag.</item><item>Show the people he travels with how to use it.</item><item>Begin a written plan for any future pain at the next visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Accessible care</content>. Accessible documents and audio and braille medicine labels are in place. [S]<list><item>Continue sending all visit summaries and results as accessible electronic documents.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Comprehensive eye examination</td><td>Yearly for blind eyes, or with symptoms</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Ophthalmology examination today: no light perception; quiet, comfortable eyes; no active disease</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Eye comfort and ocular surface check: pain, redness, discharge</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No pain, redness, or discharge; ocular surfaces quiet</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Tonometry (eye pressure)</td><td>Every glaucoma visit; for blind eyes, only if pain or redness</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No light perception and no pain; ophthalmology advises measuring only if pain or redness develops, because lowering pressure cannot help vision</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>OCT, optic disc imaging, and visual fields</td><td>Every 6 to 12 months in sighted glaucoma</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not informative without light perception</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Gonioscopy and central corneal thickness</td><td>At baseline in glaucoma</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Would not change care in blind, painless eyes</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Eye-drop adherence and side effects</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No eye drops</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Teaching about eye red flags (pain, redness, headache around the eye)</td><td>At diagnosis and each eye visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>He knows to seek same-day care for eye pain, redness, discharge, or headache around the eye</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At intake, at six months, and yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 2 four weeks ago; mood good today</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review and safety plan, with the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm; the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline is saved in his phone</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood Disorder Questionnaire</td><td>At a new depressive episode</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No depressive episode</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Recovery check: any return to use, cravings, triggers, and supports</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No return to use and no cravings; work and long-time friends remain his supports</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naloxone at home and when traveling</td><td>At intake; check expiry at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Dispensed with a braille label; one for home and one for travel</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Drug-use screen (DAST-10)</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>DAST-10 0 four weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Alcohol screen (AUDIT-C)</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>AUDIT-C 2 four weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hepatitis C, hepatitis B, and HIV testing</td><td>At entry to care for a person with a substance use disorder</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not offered at intake, a deviation from the VA/DoD guidance. He has no symptoms, so no harm is evident. Corrective action: hepatitis C, hepatitis B, and HIV testing to be drawn with his consent at the annual wellness visit, together with the Medicare preventive tests</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medication for opioid use disorder</td><td>Offered to every person with active opioid use disorder</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Sustained remission for more than thirty years without medication; he knows that buprenorphine and methadone are available if he ever needs them</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine drug testing</td><td>During treatment for active opioid use disorder</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not used in long sustained remission without treatment; it would add nothing and could feel stigmatizing</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Written plan for any future acute pain or surgery</td><td>Before any planned procedure, and once in remission</td><td>Due</td><td>To be written with him at the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Accessible documents and communication</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Spoken orientation and description of each step; visit summary sent as an accessible electronic document</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medicine labeling in braille or audio</td><td>With every new prescription</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Braille label on the naloxone; audio labels from the pharmacy</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>CDC STEADI fall-risk screen</td><td>Every visit from age 65</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No falls; steady gait; Timed Up and Go 10 seconds four weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Ophthalmology consultation</td><td>Once, then yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Vision rehabilitation services (orientation and mobility, assistive technology)</td><td>Offered to anyone with vision loss, and again with any change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Offered by ophthalmology today; he has long-established skills and does not want them now</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Advance care planning</td><td>Yearly from age 65</td><td>Due</td><td>At the annual wellness visit in the autumn</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Vaccinations</td><td>Yearly, per the CDC schedule</td><td>Due</td><td>Influenza vaccine given last autumn; others reviewed at the annual wellness visit</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Visit summaries, results, and medicine information delivered in an accessible electronic format, and medicine labels in braille or audio, by the four-week visit. Outcome by 2026-03-31: Visit summaries sent as accessible documents; the pharmacy provides audio labels and a braille label on the naloxone [S]</item><item>Naloxone at home and in his travel bag, with a tactile label, and the people he travels with shown how to use it, by the three-month visit. In progress; target 2026-06-02 [S]</item><item>Stay in sustained remission, with no return to opioid use, through six months. In progress; target 2026-09-01 [S]</item><item>No falls through six months. In progress; target 2026-09-01 [S]</item><item>A written plan for any future acute pain or surgery that uses non-opioid treatment first and builds in safeguards if an opioid is ever needed, by the six-month visit. In progress; target 2026-09-01 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Substance Use Disorders (2021); The ASAM National Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Opioid Use Disorder: 2020 Focused Update (2020); Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma Preferred Practice Pattern (2025); Vision Rehabilitation Preferred Practice Pattern (2023); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, glaucoma and depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Three-month review. Same-day visit for eye pain or redness. Yearly ophthalmology review. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-02: three-month review of recovery, accessibility, and fall prevention. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
